      "id": "diatonic-triads-in-major-keys",
      "title": "Diatonic Triads in Major Keys",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** chords | **Difficulty:** 3/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Diatonic triads use only notes from the major scale, one chord per scale degree."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pattern",
      "title": "Pattern",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "I (major), ii (minor), iii (minor), IV (major), V (major), vi (minor), vii° (diminished). In G major: G, Am, Bm, C, D, Em, F♯dim."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "primary-harmonies",
      "title": "Primary harmonies",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "I, IV, and V are the workhorses of folk song. vi (relative minor) adds emotional contrast. ii often precedes V in sophisticated progressions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "melody-mapping",
      "title": "Melody mapping",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "When guessing chords in D major, start with D, G, A, Bm. Melody notes on F♯ and A over a D bass suggest V."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
